  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Warmer and more humid conditions promote \nincreasing odds for rain Thursday \n\nShowers of varying intensity did develop during the mid to late\nafternoon in the unstable airmass along a Laurel mountains to \nI-99 corridor. These showers are dissipating as they move out of\nthe best CAPE region and the sun sets. Patchy fog is possible \ntoward morning where these showers fell, but would dissipate \nafter sunrise.\n\nA subtropical high aloft will persist over the southern Plains \nand lower MS Valley through Thursday. Around the periphery of \nthe high and in response to a shortwave trough crossing ON/QC, a\nsurface cold front will move slowly south/southeastward from \nthe lower Great Lakes across Pennsylvania and adjacent states. \nThis baroclinic/frontal zone will provide a trigger for showers \nand a few thunderstorm clusters through Thursday evening. The \nthreat for severe storms is low enough that SPC has not \nintroduced probabilities with the D2 update. WPC will maintain a\nlevel 1 excessive rain risk over the west-cetentral Alleghenies\ngiven high pwats and above normal rainfall over the past 7-10 \ndays. While it's very likely that most of central PA picks up \nsome rainfall by Thursday night, we do not anticipate a washout.\n\n\nAnother taste of Fall is on the way for the end of the week with\nnoticeably cooler and much drier/less humid air poised to return\nbehind the front (Td crashing into the 40s/50s). Expect a crisp\nand cool overnight Friday/Saturday morning with some upper 30s\npossible across the northern tier. Dry/fair wx conditions are \nforecast to start the weekend and last through the day on\nSaturday followed by periods of rain Saturday night through\nearly next week.",
